{"name":"Tulipwood","hex":"#86586a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tulipwood","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The striped, variegated wood of the tulip tree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tulipwood"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tulipwood","blocks":[{"text":"Most commonly, tulipwood is the greenish yellowish wood yielded from the tulip tree, found on the Eastern side of North America and a similar species is found in some parts of China. In the United States, it is commonly known as tulip poplar or yellow poplar, even though the tree is not related to the poplars. It is notable for its height, which can exceed 190 feet. The wood is very light, around 490 kg per cubic meter, but very strong and is used in many applications, including furniture, joinery and moldings. It can also be stained very easily and is often used as a low-cost alternative to walnut and cherry in furniture and doors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Tulip_trees_in_Douglas_Park%2C_Sleepy_Hollow_NY.jpg/330px-Tulip_trees_in_Douglas_Park%2C_Sleepy_Hollow_NY.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulipwood"},{"title":"Tulipwood (Somerset, New Jersey)","blocks":[{"text":"Tulipwood is a shingle style historic home at 1165 Hamilton Street in the Somerset section of Franklin Township, Somerset County, New Jersey, United States. It was designed by Jacob August Lienau."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Tulipwood_in_Somerset%2C_New_Jersey_on_7_November_2006%2C_image_1.jpg/330px-Tulipwood_in_Somerset%2C_New_Jersey_on_7_November_2006%2C_image_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulipwood_(Somerset%2C_New_Jersey)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/TulipwoodHough.jpg/500px-TulipwoodHough.jpg?utm_sourc